                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "golden-drop":</h3><p><b>Golden Drop</b> is the name of a process developed by Latvia&#8217;s JZ Microphones for metallizing a condenser microphone diaphragm with discrete drops of gold (or gold alloy), rather than coating the entire surface of the diaphagm evenly.</p><p>According to JZ, the advantages are:</p><p>Dynamic Detail:</p><blockquote><p><cite>JZ</cite></p><p>The Golden Drops diaphragm has reduced mass when compared with the same size diaphragm without Golden Drop technology. This reduced inertia allows Golden Drops capsules to detect and respond faster. Faster detection delivers significantly more detailed dynamic information to your recordings.</p></blockquote><p>Clarity:</p><blockquote><p><cite>JZ</cite></p><p>The Golden Drops diaphragm gives more clarity, more precision, less coloration and less frequency response distortion compared to the same size diaphragm without Golden Drop technology.</p></blockquote><p>As of mid-2010, there are five distinct JZ capsules with Golden Drops diaphragms:</p><ul><li><b><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/gdc1">GDC1</a></b> - a 27mm single-diaphragm model found in the BH2 and BH3</li><li><b><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/gdc2">GDC2</a></b> - a pair of single-diaphragm capsules (perhaps identical to the GDC1), mounted back-to-back. This multipattern capsule is used in the BH1 and BH1S.</li><li><b><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/gdc3">GDC3</a></b> - a 21mm, center-terminated design used in the BT-301.</li><li><b><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/gdc47">GDC47</a></b> - a 25mm dual-diaphragm capsule with a single, shared backplate; used in the Vintage V47</li><li><b><a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/gdc67">GDC67</a></b> - a dual-diaphragm, dual-backplate capsule  found in the V67.</li><li><b>GDC12</b> - a new capsule from the Vintage series, for the V12 (AKG C12 heritage).</li></ul><p></p>
